    <DataNode elementId="CPD-8838" textLabel="juvenile hormone III" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="QVJMXSGZTCGLHZ-HONBPKQLSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="chawkins, SRI, caspi">Several compounds with juvenile hormone (JH) activity have been isolated from insects. The first three were named |FRAME: CPD-17897| (JH1), |FRAME: METHYL-EPOXYTRIDECADIENOATE| (JH2), and |FRAME: CPD-8838| (JH3). The three compounds differ widely in their physiological activity and their hemolymph concentrations during the insect&apos;s life cycle. JH3 is the predominant hormone in most adult insects, while JHI and JH2 have been identified mainly in larvae of Lepidoptera and in nymphs of |FRAME: TAX-6990|.</Comment>
